I found some inconsistencies in Chapter 25, section 25.2.4, regarding the definition of the Euclidian distance.

## Originals for comparison

```R
pdistR <- function(x, ys) {
  sqrt((x - ys) ^ 2)
}

cppFunction('NumericVector pdistC(double x, NumericVector ys) {
  int n = ys.size();
  NumericVector out(n);

  for(int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
    out[i] = sqrt(pow(ys[i] - x, 2.0));
  }
  return out;
}')
```

## My suggestion

Arguably,  to be a distance, a function must map from a space in `n` dimensions to a space in one dimension. 
Since the chapter uses the `l2` norm, then the correct formula for a space in `n` dimensions would be:

```R 
pdistR <- function(x, ys) {
  sqrt(sum( (x - ys) ^ 2 ) )
}
```
Even if the point is to highlight the ambiguity that the original R function has
(i.e., we don't know if it applies to a scalar or vector), the ambiguity in fact does not exist,
since the original function is incorrect for vectors.

This also suggests the alteration for the C++ function, since
the function is returning a vector instead of a scalar:

```R
cppFunction('double pdistC(double x, NumericVector ys) {
  int n = ys.size();
  double out(n) = 0;

  for(int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
    out += pow(ys[i] - x, 2.0);
  }

  return sqrt(out);
}')
```
Also, the distance is implicitly calculating not the distance to a value, but
the distance to a point where all the dimensions have the same value.
It would be better to be explicit and require a comparison between two vectors:

```R
cppFunction('double pdistC(NumericVector x, NumericVector ys) {

  int n_x = x.size();
  int n_y = ys.size();
  double out = 0;

  if(n_x != n_y) {   
   stop("Vectors must have the same size.");
  } 
  else {
    for(int i = 0; i < n_y; ++i) {
      out += pow(ys[i] - x[i], 2.0);
    }
    return sqrt(out);
 }
}')
```

