In the passage about how `bool` does not handle `NA` values

> While C++'s `bool` has two possible values (`true` or `false`), a logical vector in R has three (`TRUE`, `FALSE`, and `NA`). If you coerce a length 1 logical vector, make sure it doesn't contain any missing values otherwise they will be converted to TRUE.

it might be helpful to suggest a way to grow a LogicalVector efficiently.  In this case, `LogicalVector x; x.push_back(NA_LOGICAL);` is slow, and `std::vector<bool> x; x.push_back(NA_LOGICAL);` is incorrect.

In the Rcpp Gallery, you [wrote](http://gallery.rcpp.org/articles/working-with-missing-values/) 

> To work with missing values in logical vectors, use an int instead of a bool.

I think this would benefit by an example of growing a `std::vector<int>`, later converting it into a `LogicalVector`.  E.g. 

``` r
Rcpp::cppFunction("
LogicalVector missing_logical() {
  std::vector<int> vint;
  vint.push_back(true);
  vint.push_back(false);
  // Any type of NA_* works, except NA_STRING
  vint.push_back(NA_LOGICAL);
  vint.push_back(NA_INTEGER);
  vint.push_back(NA_REAL);
  // Either, 1.  Convert to LogicalVector ...
  LogicalVector vlog = wrap(vint);
  // ... continue with the LogicalVector, then finally ...
  return(vlog);
  // Or, 2. Convert and return in one step
  // return wrap(vint);
}
")
missing_logical()
#> [1]  TRUE FALSE    NA    NA    NA
```

