The find_assign4 function defined on pp. 306-307 (used to find names of all objects that have been assigned values) reproduced below

```
find_assign4 <- function(x) {
if (is.atomic(x) || is.name(x)) {
character()
} else if (is.call(x)) {
if (identical(x[[1]], quote(`<-`)) && is.name(x[[2]])) {
lhs <- as.character(x[[2]])
} else {
lhs <- character()
}
unique(c(lhs, unlist(lapply(x, find_assign4))))
} else if (is.pairlist(x)) {
unique(unlist(lapply(x, find_assign4)))
} else {
stop("Don't know how to handle type ", typeof(x),
call. = FALSE)
}}

```

The function is tested (p. 307)

```
find_assign4(quote({
l <- list()
l$a <- 5
names(l) <- "b"
}))
#> [1] "l"
```

This works fine, though I think this is because the function works in finding the binding name l from  l <- list().  If we instead run

```
find_assign4(quote({
l$a <- 5
names(l) <- "b"
}))
#>  character(0)

```
which of course should be "l". Take the case l$a<-b,  I think in find_assign4 when we assign b to l$a we call the function '$' so x[[2]] will not be a name but the language object l$a. As the function is written above it would skip past this and never go back to delve deeper in to the expression tree for l$a to pull out "l", hence yielding character(0) as the final output unless some other part of the quote had a binding to an object that didn't perform another call (e.g l<-list()). I think instead we need to check if x[[2]] is not a name but is a call (in this example'$'), then  return x[[2]][[2]] if it is a name object?

This seems to fix it anyway as if we define

```
find_assign5 <- function(x) {
if (is.atomic(x) || is.name(x)) {
character()
} else if (is.call(x)) {
if (identical(x[[1]], quote(`<-`)) && is.name(x[[2]])) {
lhs <- as.character(x[[2]])
}  else if (identical(x[[1]], quote(`<-`)) && is.call(x[[2]]) && is.name(x[[2]][[2]])) {
lhs<-as.character(x[[2]][[2]])}     
else {
lhs <- character()
}       
unique(c(lhs, unlist(lapply(x, find_assign5))))
} else if (is.pairlist(x)) {
unique(unlist(lapply(x, find_assign5)))
} else {
stop("Don't know how to handle type ", typeof(x),
call. = FALSE)
}}   
```                                                         


```
find_assign5(quote({
l$a <- 5
names(l) <- "b"
 }))
  #>  "l"
```

